# What is fixed in the short run?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Short run versus long run Why a firm may keep running at a loss"
next: {"title":"Why are some industries dominated by giants?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/111-why-are-some-industries-dominated-by.cast"}

## Pumping at a loss
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Oil price
      - Drilling rigs
      - Oil pumped
    values:
      - 55
      - 22
      - 98
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    y_label: May 2016, Oct 2014 = 100
    icons:
      - money bag
      - construction
      - oil drum
    colors:
      - orange
      - red
      - green
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    reference:
      value: 100
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 170
      w: 560
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: eia2016
      title: Oil and natural gas sector employment declines, but less than rig counts (Today in Energy)
      authors: U.S. Energy Information Administration
      year: 2016
      finding: Total US rig count fell from nearly 1,800 in fall 2014 to a weekly low of 404 in May 2016; May 2016 crude oil production was 2% below October 2014.
      url: https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=27392
    - id: fredwti
      title: 'Crude Oil Prices: West Texas Intermediate (WTI), monthly (MCOILWTICO)'
      authors: U.S. EIA via FRED, Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis
      year: 2016
      finding: Monthly average WTI price $84.40 in October 2014 and $46.71 in May 2016 (55%).
      url: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/MCOILWTICO

    icon well size 100 set twemoji x 830 y 560 or ["factory"] icon_look picture of "oil drum" hidden true
    text sunk "drilling: sunk" #b5482e x 830 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text pump "pumping: covered" #2e7d32 x 830 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sr "Short run: wells fixed" x 830 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    text lr "Long run: drill or not?" x 830 y 460 font_size 30 hidden true
    text src "Data: EIA, FRED" #777 cites ["eia2016","fredwti"] x 830 y 200 font_size 18 hidden true

    card title "Pumping at a loss"

What is fixed in the short run, and why would a firm keep working while it loses money? Look at American oil.
    draw axes reference_1 src

B: Between the a (@draw bar_1@)utumn of 2014 and the spring of 2016, the price of a barrel roughly halved.

Drillers answ (@draw bar_2@)ered at once. The rigs drilling for oil and gas fell by almost four-fifths.

B: Then the oil coming out of the ground must have collapsed too.
    highlight bar_2

    ask "Rigs fell by almost four-fifths. Drag the 'Oil pumped' bar to your guess." on bar_3 store g default "40" pool false
        right: Ninety-eight: the oil kept flowing.
        wrong: You said {g:0}. The oil kept flowing: here, ninety-eight.

Hardly at all: the oil pumped fell by about two per cent.
    highlight bar_3 effect circle

B: Two per cent? With the price halved, they must have been pumping at a loss.
    highlight bar_1

Counting what the wells cost to drill, m (@draw well@)any were. But that money was spent, whatever they did next.

Economists call it a (@draw sunk@) sunk cost: spent, and impossible to get back.

B: And pumping from a well that's already drilled costs far less than (@draw pump@) drilling one. The price still covered that.

That's the short run: a stretch (@draw sr@) when some things can't be changed, here the wells. The long run is w (@draw lr@)hen everything can, including whether to drill at all.
    erase well sunk pump

B: So it isn't a time on the clock. A bakery's short run might be months; an oil field's, years.
    highlight sr lr

So the drillers cut what they could still choose, and kept pumping what was already paid for.
    highlight bar_2 bar_3

## Open or close?
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: competition
    show:
      - mc
      - atc
      - avc
      - price_line
    price: 45
    shade: loss
    fixed_cost: 400
    mark_minima: true
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      price_unit: $
    price_tick: true
    price_label: P = {p}
    x_label: Loaves a day
    y_label: $
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 490

    icon shop size 110 set twemoji x 830 y 500 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    text rent "Rent: $40 a day" #b5482e x 830 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text rent2 "open or shut" #b5482e x 830 y 515 font_size 26 hidden true
    text open "Open: −$19 a day" x 830 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true
    text shut "Shut: −$40 a day" x 830 y 380 font_size 32 hidden true
    text toward "≈ $20 toward rent" #2e7d32 x 830 y 320 font_size 28 hidden true
    text rule "Short run: open while P ≥ AVC" #1f5fa8 x 830 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text avcnum "bottom of AVC: $2.55" #1f5fa8 x 830 y 430 font_size 24 hidden true
    text lrule "Long run: stay if P ≥ ATC" #b5482e x 830 y 360 font_size 28 hidden true
    text atcnum "bottom of ATC: $3.40" #b5482e x 830 y 320 font_size 24 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"rejoin"}] then rejoin hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\text{Open: } (P - AVC)\\,Q - F"},{"tex":"\\text{Shut: } -F"},{"tex":"\\text{Open wins if } P > AVC"}] x 840 y 250 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Open or close?"

    hide shade label_shade q_star label_q_star p_star label_p_star min_avc label_min_avc min_atc label_min_atc

A well already drilled keeps pumping while the oil pays for the pumping. Mia's bakery in Harbourtown does the same sum every winter.
    draw axes shop

B: Here are her costs, with illustr (@draw mc label_mc@)ative numbers: MC, the cost of one more (@draw atc label_atc@) loaf, and ATC, the average cost of a loaf. And AVC, th (@draw avc label_avc@)e average cost without the rent.

Why leave the rent out?
    erase shop

B: Because she signed a lease for the year: forty d (@draw rent rent2@)ollars a day, open or shut. It's fixed, since it doesn't change (@highlight atc avc@) with the loaves, and for this winter it's sunk.

B: When the to (@draw price_line label_price_line price_tick label_price_tick@)urists leave, the price of a loaf f (@animate price 30 duration 2.5@)alls to, say, three dollars, below the bottom of ATC at three forty.

So she loses  (@draw shade label_shade q_star@)money on every loaf. Shouldn't she close until spring?

    quiz "Winter: should Mia open or close?" pool false
        right: Stay open: the price covers her flour and labour, and the rent is due either way.
        wrong: Closed, the rent is still due, and the price covers more than the flour and labour: here, stay open.
        * Close: every loaf loses money
        + Stay open
        * It makes no difference

B: Open, she  (@draw open@)loses about nineteen dollars a day. Shut, s (@draw shut@)he still owes the rent, so she loses forty.

B: Three dollars covers the flour, the power and her assistant, with ab (@draw toward@)out twenty dollars left over toward the rent.

So the curve that matters this wint (@highlight avc effect pulse@)er isn't ATC. It's AVC.
    erase open shut toward rent rent2

B: Right. Below two fifty-five (@draw min_avc@), th (@hide label_min_avc@)e bottom of AVC, a loaf doesn't even pay for its own flour and labour. That's the shutdown rule:  (@draw rule avcnum@)stay open while the price covers average variable cost.

B: Try it: drag the price between two fifty-five and three forty, and watch the loss stay below the forty dollars of rent.
    explore params ["price"]

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the sum behind the rule?

@math

    erase mathq

Open, her profit is P minus AVC, times Q, minus the rent F.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Shut, she sells nothing and still pays F.
    draw math_line_2

F is on both sides, so it cancels: open wins whenever P is above AVC.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@rejoin

    erase mathq

    animate price 30 duration 1

And in s (@draw min_atc@)p (@hide label_min_atc@)ring, when the lease runs out?

B: Then the rent isn't sunk any more; it's a choice again. In the (@draw lrule atcnum@) long run Mia stays only if the price cover (@highlight min_atc effect pulse@)s ATC: three forty.

## Why sunk costs still pull
sources:
    - id: arkes1985
      title: The psychology of sunk cost
      authors: Hal R. Arkes and Catherine Blumer
      year: 1985
      finding: The first 60 buyers of 1982–83 Ohio University Theater season tickets were randomly charged $15, $13 or $8; full-price buyers attended more plays in the first half of the season.
    - id: dawkins1976
      title: Parental investment, mate desertion and a fallacy (Nature 262)
      authors: Richard Dawkins and Tamsin R. Carlisle
      year: 1976
      finding: 'Named the ''Concorde fallacy'': judging by past investment rather than future benefit, after the British–French supersonic jet.'
    - id: dixit1989
      title: Entry and exit decisions under uncertainty (Journal of Political Economy 97)
      authors: Avinash Dixit
      year: 1989
      finding: 'With sunk entry/exit costs and uncertain prices, an active firm stays until the price falls below variable cost minus interest on the exit cost: hysteresis.'
    - id: aks2018
      title: Hotelling under pressure (Journal of Political Economy 126)
      authors: Soren T. Anderson, Ryan Kellogg and Stephen W. Salant
      year: 2018
      finding: Oil production from existing Texas wells does not respond to price; drilling activity and costs respond strongly.
    - id: friedman2007
      title: Searching for the sunk cost fallacy (Experimental Economics 10)
      authors: Daniel Friedman, Kai Pommerenke, Rajan Lukose, Garrett Milam and Bernardo Huberman
      year: 2007
      finding: In a lab search game the sunk cost effect was surprisingly small and insensitive to the proposed psychological drivers.

    text hl "Sunk costs pull" x 270 y 610 font_size 38 hidden true
    text hr "Staying can pay" x 750 y 610 font_size 38 hidden true
    path divider points [[520,650],[520,250]] style.stroke #999 hidden true
    icon ticket size 100 set twemoji x 110 y 500 or ["admission tickets"] icon_look picture of ticket hidden true
    text t15 "$15" #b5482e x 230 y 500 font_size 40 hidden true
    text t13 "$13" x 330 y 500 font_size 40 hidden true
    text t8 "$8" x 420 y 500 font_size 40 hidden true
    text went "paid more → went more" #b5482e cites ["arkes1985"] x 280 y 425 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon plane size 100 set twemoji x 110 y 300 icon_look picture of airplane hidden true
    text conc "Concorde fallacy" cites ["dawkins1976"] x 310 y 305 font_size 38 hidden true
    text dixit "Restarting costs too" cites ["dixit1989"] x 750 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text dixit2 "Dixit, 1989" #777 x 750 y 450 font_size 26 hidden true
    text costs "crew · customers · restart" #2e7d32 x 750 y 370 font_size 30 hidden true
    text s1 "Ignore sunk costs: consensus" #2e7d32 x 500 y 540 font_size 46 hidden true
    text s2 "Firms do: mostly agreed" #1f5fa8 cites ["aks2018"] x 500 y 430 font_size 46 hidden true
    text s3 "People do: contested" #c0392b cites ["friedman2007"] x 500 y 320 font_size 46 hidden true

    card title "Why sunk costs still pull"

Ignore what's sunk: that rule kept the  (@draw hl divider@)wells pumping and Mia's ovens hot. But do people follow it?

B: Not reliably. In 1982 Hal Arkes and Catherine Blumer  (@draw ticket@)had an Ohio University theatre sell season tickets at three prices, chose (@draw t15@)n at random: fifte (@draw t13@)en dollars, thirt (@draw t8@)een, or eight.

Same plays, same seats. Only the money already spent was different.
    highlight t15 t13 t8

B: In the first half of the season the full-p (@draw went@)rice buyers went to more plays. The money was gone either (@highlight t15@) way, yet it pulled them out of the house.

Governments do it on a grand scale. Britain and F (@draw plane@)rance kept paying for Concorde long after it was clear it would nev (@draw conc@)er pay its way, and biologists named the error after it: the Concorde fallacy.

B: But don't call every (@draw hr@) stubborn firm a fool. Avinash Dixit showed that when prices jump ab (@draw dixit@)out and restarting is costly, a firm can be right (@draw dixit2@) to stay open even when the price dips a little below AVC.

Because closing has costs of its own:  (@draw costs@)a crew that leaves, customers who go elsewhere, a costly restart. Waiting can be worth it.

B: How sure are we? That sunk costs should not steer a choice (@draw s1@): consensus. That producers behave that way: mostly agree (@draw s2@)d. In Texas, oil from existing wells barely responds to price; drilling does.
    erase hl hr divider ticket t15 t13 t8 went plane conc dixit dixit2 costs

That people fall for  (@draw s3@)the fallacy: contested. In careful lab games the effect turned out small and patchy.

    quiz "A ski resort has a warm winter. Lift tickets bring in $8,000 a day; staff and power for the lifts cost $6,000 a day; the loan on the lifts costs $5,000 a day, open or shut. Open for the season?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Yes: open it loses $3,000 a day, shut it loses the whole $5,000 loan payment.
        wrong: Compare the loss open with the loss shut, where the loan is still due: here, stay open.
        * No: it loses $3,000 a day
        + Yes: it loses less than shut
        * Only if the loan is paid off

    erase s1 s2 s3

    cards cinema bins ["Counts tonight","Sunk: ignore"] check each
        Counts tonight: Projectionist's pay tonight, Heating the hall tonight
        * "Last year's renovation" bin "Sunk: ignore"
        * "This month's film licence, paid" bin "Sunk: ignore"

    ask "Counts tonight, or sunk? Sort each cost." on cinema store c stage own voice b
        right: All four: tonight's pay and heat are saved by not screening; the renovation and the licence are gone either way.
        wrong: {c} of {c.total} right. Ask of each: is it saved if the screen stays dark? Here, only tonight's pay and heat.

    erase cinema

    draw s1 s2 s3

    quiz "A fisher's catch pays for fuel and crew but not her boat loan. The boat is worn out and must be replaced. Buy a new one?"
        right: Not at today's prices: before she buys it, the new boat isn't sunk, so it must pay its way like everything else.
        wrong: Before she buys it, is the new boat's cost sunk? It isn't: here, not at today's prices.
        * Yes: the catch covers the running costs
        + Not at today's prices: the new boat is a choice
        * Yes: otherwise the old loan was wasted

So what is fixed in the short run? Whatever is already sunk. The skill is to stop counting it, and to see when the long run turns it back into a choice.
    highlight s1 effect box

